  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Katya simply swipes the spare key from her father's office and leads Max into the server room, while Nagiyev pays movers to haul away his uncomfortable sofa. Listen to the full scene first, and then we will examine each line.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "У меня тоже хороший план есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Пойдём скорее улики уничтожать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Падать с этого дивана очень больно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "И спать на нём одиноко.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Взяли диван и понесли отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Тёплый плед вам в подарок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "В общем, шесть бутылок дорогого алкоголя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Три из них разбили, три унесли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Пойдём смотреть записи с камер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Как мы это стирать будем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, я сейчас вернусь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Куда ты сейчас побежал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне срочно нужно в туалет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Тоже мне, нашёл время!",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "У меня тоже хороший план есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have a good plan too.' In Russian, the idea of 'at' flows naturally into 'there is' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Пойдём скорее улики уничтожать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's go destroy the evidence quickly.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'let's go' and finishing on 'to destroy'. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Падать с этого дивана очень больно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Falling off this sofa hurts a lot.' Notice how Russian expresses 'to fall' and 'painful' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "И спать на нём одиноко.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And sleeping on it is lonely.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'lonely'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Взяли диван и понесли отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Pick up the sofa and carry it out of here.' Dmitry Nagiyev brings out the contrast between 'took' and 'sofa' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Тёплый плед вам в подарок.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The warm blanket is a gift for you.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'warm' before landing on 'gift'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "В общем, шесть бутылок дорогого алкоголя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In short, six bottles of expensive alcohol.' The grammar packs 'shared' and 'of alcohol' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Три из них разбили, три унесли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Three of them were smashed, three were carried off.' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'to carry away' covers a group or the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Пойдём смотреть записи с камер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's go watch the camera recordings.' In Russian, the idea of 'let's go' flows naturally into 'cameras' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Как мы это стирать будем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How are we going to erase this?' In Russian, the idea of 'how' flows naturally into 'will' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, я сейчас вернусь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, I will be right back.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'wait' and finishing on 'will return'. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Куда ты сейчас побежал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where did you run off to right now?' Katya anchors the line on 'where to', while 'ran' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне срочно нужно в туалет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I urgently need the restroom.' Max brings out the contrast between 'to me' and 'urgently' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "katya",
      "t": "Тоже мне, нашёл время!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Great, you picked a fine time!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'to find'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
